    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d23">

                <pb facs="0136" n="32" sameAs="frus:frus1890/pg32"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Mizner to Mr.
                            Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Guatemala</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1890-07-16"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 16, 1890</date>.
                        (Received July 19.)</dateline></opener>
                <p>Mr. Mizner reports that he is informed by the Government of Guatemala that a
                    Pacific mail steamer which left San Francisco on the 3d instant, carrying ten
                    thousand stand of small arms for Salvador, is expected to arrive at San José de
                    Guatemala on the 17th instant.</p>
                <p>Mr. Mizner says that the Guatemalan Government appeals to that <pb facs="0137"
                        n="33" xml:id="pg33"/> of the United States to cause the steamer to carry
                    the arms beyond Salvadorian territory and land them in some port of a neutral
                    State until consultation between the Guatemalan authorities and the ministers of
                    Nicaragua and Costa Rica at Guatemala. He asks that he may be immediately
                    instructed, and says that the steamer is detained until the 20th instant. He
                    expresses the belief that, if there be no remedy, Guatemala may, for the purpose
                    of claiming the arms as contraband, formally declare war, and he asks whether
                    the arms in that case can be taken from an American vessel.</p>
            </div>
